/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 49 0 R
/E (Decentralised sanitation is the part of India's wastewater management sector that serves locations where conventional sewerage is unavailable, uneconomical, or unable to keep pace with development. Tier 2 and Tier 3 urbanisation has expanded this need across residential, hospitality, educational, commercial and institutional developments. Unlike municipal sanitation, this segment is privately funded. Developers, businesses, institutions and individual property owners pay for and operate their own infrastructure, either through direct procurement or CSR eligible spending, since sanitation and safe drinking water are recognised Schedule VII CSR activities under the Companies Act.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
91 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 49 0 R
/E (A few structural tailwinds are worth noting: construction activity spreading beyond metros, tightening environmental compliance, groundwater contamination risk from poorly maintained septic systems, and a growing preference for maintenance free, sludge free on site treatment. EPBL's bio digester systems are typically configured for 700 to 15,000 litre applications and STP projects range from 5 Kilo Litres per Day \(KLD\) to Million Litres per Day \(MLD\) which makes them most relevant to properties without a municipal sewer connection.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
